# Territory Grounder — code ownership of the "law" surfaces.
#
# These files are the guardrails an autonomous coding agent operates UNDER; per SDD best practice
# (ADR-0009, finding (ii): "if it must happen, it belongs in a gate the agent cannot bypass"), changes
# to them require an explicit human code-owner review rather than being silently rewritten by an agent.
#
# ENFORCEMENT (TG-185). This file used to say "until then this file is advisory", and that sentence
# outlived the condition it described: the `protected-paths` CI job now runs scripts/lint-protected-paths.sh
# on every merge request, it carries no allow_failure, and it REFUSES the merge when a path listed below
# changes without a `Law-Change-Approved-By: @<codeowners-handle>` trailer naming an owner from THIS file.
# The gate has its own oracle (scripts/lint-protected-paths_test.sh) running in the same job, so a gate
# that stopped examining anything fails rather than passing quietly.
#
# Two things that sentence still gets right, kept because they are the remaining gap and not a
# formality: this is a REPO-side gate, so it binds anything that goes through CI and does not bind a
# direct push by someone who can bypass CI. Enabling "Require approval from Code Owners" on the
# protected `main` branch is what closes that last hole, and it is a GitLab setting rather than
# something this file can assert.
#
# The distinction matters more here than in most repositories: these are the guardrails an autonomous
# agent operates under, and the agent can read this file. A control that describes itself as advisory
# invites being treated as advisory.

